\section{Solving a system of differential equations numerically} Once the construction of a system dynamics model is complete, it is necessary to define the elements and variables involved in the equations of this system. This task relies on the data obtained from the available statistics on the factors and significant variables of the system \cite{b10}. After determining the functional dependencies and coefficients in the equations, and external factors, the resulting system of equations is solved by the numerical methods of the fourth order of accuracy.
Figure \ref{Rezchikov_fig4} shows the change in time of the calculated values for all calculated values of the model variables over the interval from 2021 to 2024.
\begin{figure}[h!] \renewcommand{\figurename}{Fig.} \centering \includegraphics[width=110mm]{Fig4.jpg}\\ \caption{Forecasting results for each variable for 2021--2024 (color online)} \label{Rezchikov_fig4} \end{figure}
We compared the calculation results with actual statistical data for each variable. For example, let us present graphs comparing statistical and simulated data for 2004--2010 for variables $X_2$ and $X_8$. The results are shown in Fig.~\ref{Rezchikov_fig5}, respectively.
In Fig.~\ref{Rezchikov_fig5} \textit{a}, the maximum deviation from statistics was 9.8 percent in 2009; in Fig.~\ref{Rezchikov_fig5} \textit{b}, it was 10 percent in 2010.
